The hot cost in `parse1` is `isDelim(c)`, which calls `BitSet.get`. That call does bounds and word-index checks, and for chars above 127 it reads beyond the set's words. The delimiter set only contains ASCII characters: 0x09, 0x20-0x2F, 0x3B-0x40, 0x5B-0x60 and 0x7B-0x7E. So I replaced the lookup with inline range comparisons, which behave identically and need no memory load. Non-ASCII characters (c >= 0x80) are not delimiters, which matches the BitSet, where those bits are unset.

I wrote the check as a single condition: `c == 9 || (c >= 0x20 && c <= 0x2F) || (c >= 0x3B && c <= 0x40) || (c >= 0x5B && c <= 0x60) || (c >= 0x7B && c <= 0x7E)`. A 128-bit mask in two longs would need constants, which I can declare locally as `final long` values. That gives a branch-light test:

- Low mask, bits 0-63: bit 9, bits 0x20-0x2F (32-47) and bits 0x3B-0x3F (59-63).
- High mask, bits 64-127, indexed by `c - 64`: bit 0x40 (index 0), bits 0x5B-0x60 (indices 27-32) and bits 0x7B-0x7E (indices 59-62).

Computing these constants by hand risks errors, so I used the range comparisons, which are simple and correct. I also hoisted `isDelim` out of the helper call, though the JIT would probably inline it anyway.


