public static int unsignedUnion2by2(
      final char[] set1,
      final int offset1,
      final int length1,
      final char[] set2,
      final int offset2,
      final int length2,
      final char[] buffer) {
    if (0 == length2) {
      System.arraycopy(set1, offset1, buffer, 0, length1);
      return length1;
    }
    if (0 == length1) {
      System.arraycopy(set2, offset2, buffer, 0, length2);
      return length2;
    }
    final int end1 = length1 + offset1;
    final int end2 = length2 + offset2;
    int pos = 0;
    int k1 = offset1, k2 = offset2;
    char s1 = set1[k1];
    char s2 = set2[k2];
    while (true) {
      if (s1 < s2) {
        buffer[pos++] = s1;
        ++k1;
        if (k1 >= end1) {
          System.arraycopy(set2, k2, buffer, pos, end2 - k2);
          return pos + end2 - k2;
        }
        s1 = set1[k1];
      } else if (s1 == s2) {
        buffer[pos++] = s1;
        ++k1;
        ++k2;
        if (k1 >= end1) {
          System.arraycopy(set2, k2, buffer, pos, end2 - k2);
          return pos + end2 - k2;
        }
        if (k2 >= end2) {
          System.arraycopy(set1, k1, buffer, pos, end1 - k1);
          return pos + end1 - k1;
        }
        s1 = set1[k1];
        s2 = set2[k2];
      } else {
        buffer[pos++] = s2;
        ++k2;
        if (k2 >= end2) {
          System.arraycopy(set1, k1, buffer, pos, end1 - k1);
          return pos + end1 - k1;
        }
        s2 = set2[k2];
      }
    }
  }

text:
Hoisting the end indices out of the loop is the only change I'd make. The original recomputes `length1 + offset1` and `length2 + offset2` on every iteration, and the JIT may already fold that, but making it explicit is cheap and safe.

The tail copies must keep the exact original arithmetic. For example, `length2 - k2 + offset2` equals `end2 - k2`, so each tail length is `end - k` and the return value is `pos + (end - k)`. The two forms are the same integer expression, so overflow behaves identically as well.

I did not change the structure of the merge loop itself.

