text:
The hot cost here is `isDelim(c)`, which calls `BitSet.get`. That involves a bounds check, a word-index computation, and a possible array-length check for each character. I replaced it with inline range comparisons that are equivalent to the delimiter set.

The delimiter set is 0x09, 0x20-0x2F, 0x3B-0x40, 0x5B-0x60 and 0x7B-0x7E.

Characters above 0x7E are not delimiters. `BitSet.get` on a large index returns false, so the inline check matches that. Characters below 0x20 other than 0x09 are not delimiters either.

I use a switch-free chain of comparisons, with an early out for `c > 0x7E`. The inline check is:

- `c >= 0x7F`: not a delimiter.
- `c >= 0x7B`: delimiter.
- `c >= 0x5B`: delimiter if `c <= 0x60`.
- `c >= 0x3B`: delimiter if `c <= 0x40`.
- `c >= 0x20`: delimiter if `c <= 0x2F`.
- Otherwise: delimiter only if `c == 0x09`.

A character in 0x30-0x3A (digits and ':') is not a delimiter. Letters 0x41-0x5A and 0x61-0x7A are not delimiters.

The ordering of the checks is important. Putting the common letter and digit cases first would be faster, but correctness is the priority, so I kept it simple with a boolean computed per character.


