Two changes: build the result directly in a TreeMap, and avoid the double lookup. Building a HashMap and then copying it into a TreeMap costs an extra pass and extra allocation. The original already boxes `ra.keys[i]` through a `Character` conversion, so `Character.valueOf` is still used.

The TreeMap is created with the natural ordering of `Character`, which matches the original. A `get` followed by a `put` costs two tree traversals, whereas a HashMap needs two hash lookups. The tree has at most 65536 keys, so each traversal is O(log n). The rewrite keeps `get` and `put` on the TreeMap, but only when a key is missing. A cache of the last key would avoid even that: bitmaps are sorted by key, so consecutive keys differ, which makes such a cache of little use.

A HashMap is O(1) per lookup against O(log n) for a TreeMap. For many bitmaps with many containers, the HashMap lookup is cheaper, and the final TreeMap copy built from a HashMap is a sort. So the best choice depends on the distribution of keys, and the swap may not be a clear win. A safer improvement keeps the HashMap and drops the redundant work. The TreeMap constructor from a HashMap does a `putAll` of a non-sorted map, which inserts n entries, so that cost remains either way.

I keep the HashMap, size it to avoid rehashing, and use the `Character` key only once per entry.

