public static SortedMap<Character, List<Container>> groupByKey(RoaringBitmap... bitmaps) {
    int total = 0;
    for (RoaringBitmap bitmap : bitmaps) {
      total += bitmap.highLowContainer.size;
    }
    int expected = Math.min(total, 1 << 16);
    Map<Character, List<Container>> grouped = new HashMap<>((int) (expected / 0.75f) + 1);
    for (RoaringBitmap bitmap : bitmaps) {
      RoaringArray ra = bitmap.highLowContainer;
      int size = ra.size;
      char[] keys = ra.keys;
      Container[] values = ra.values;
      for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        Character key = keys[i];
        List<Container> slice = grouped.get(key);
        if (null == slice) {
          slice = new ArrayList<>();
          grouped.put(key, slice);
        }
        slice.add(values[i]);
      }
    }
    return new TreeMap<>(grouped);
  }

text:
Two changes: build the grouping in a TreeMap directly, and avoid the redundant lookups and the final copy.

- The original builds a HashMap, then copies it into a TreeMap, which sorts and allocates again.
- The original does `get` followed by `put`, which is two hash lookups on a miss.
- Keys are chars, so `Character.valueOf` hits the cache only for values up to 127. Boxing happens once per container either way.

A TreeMap used directly would do O(log n) comparisons per insert, which can cost more than hashing. I considered a char-indexed array of 65536 lists instead, but that costs 256KB per call, which is bad for small inputs.

I kept the HashMap and made three smaller changes:
- Presize the HashMap, with an upper bound based on the total container count capped at 65536.
- Use `computeIfAbsent`-free logic with a single `get`, boxing the key once.
- Build the TreeMap from the HashMap as before. Passing a HashMap to `new TreeMap<>(Map)` is not a SortedMap, so it goes through `putAll`, which is O(n log n) inserts. Sorting the keys first and filling the TreeMap in order won't help either, because `putAll` on a non-sorted map is the only available path. Using `new TreeMap<>()` with `put` in sorted order is O(n) per insert at best, with no gain.

The presizing avoids HashMap rehashing, which is the clearest measurable gain. The `ArrayList` slices keep the default capacity.


