@Override
  public MutableRoaringArray clone() {
    MutableRoaringArray sa;
    try {
      sa = (MutableRoaringArray) super.clone();

      final int n = this.size;
      final MappeableContainer[] src = this.values;
      sa.keys = Arrays.copyOf(this.keys, n);
      final MappeableContainer[] dst = new MappeableContainer[n];
      for (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {
        dst[k] = src[k].clone();
      }
      sa.values = dst;
      sa.size = n;
      return sa;

    } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
      return null;
    }
  }

text:
The `values` array copy is redundant because every element is overwritten with a clone. I replaced it with a fresh `MappeableContainer[]` that is filled directly with `this.values[k].clone()`. This skips copying the array contents, and it avoids reading each element back from the new array. The `keys` copy and the `size` handling stay as they were.

`Arrays.copyOf(values, size)` returns an array of the runtime type of `values`, which is `MappeableContainer[]` because the field is declared that way and always initialised with that type. A plain `new MappeableContainer[size]` therefore has the same type.

The saving is small, since the copied array is only a memcpy of references. Behaviour is otherwise identical.

